(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-glaucoma-diagnostics-quiz"]={"title":"Glaucoma & Diagnostic Agents Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which systemic adverse effects can a topical ophthalmic beta blocker produce?","choices":["Bradycardia, heart block and bronchoconstriction","Hypertension and tachycardia","Central nervous system depression and apnoea","Bitter taste and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A drop is not a local-only treatment — nasolacrimal absorption avoids first-pass metabolism, so a topical beta blocker can do everything a systemic one does. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"How do atropine, cyclopentolate and tropicamide produce mydriasis?","choices":["They activate alpha-1 adrenergic receptors","They competitively block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors","They activate muscarinic receptors on the iris dilator","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking muscarinic receptors is the exact opposite of pilocarpine's action, which is why one dilates and the other constricts. They are used diagnostically and in uveitis to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A patient using latanoprost thinks two drops a night will lower the pressure further. What should they be told?","choices":["Two drops a night is acceptable if the pressure is not at target","It is harmless but wasteful","Exceeding once daily dosing inhibits the pressure-lowering effect","It should be split to morning and evening instead"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is counter-intuitive and therefore a good question: more drug does not mean more effect, it means less. Once daily is the maximum as well as the minimum. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which glaucoma causes blindness?","choices":["Raised pressure causes corneal oedema and then scarring","Raised pressure causes cataract formation","Optic neuropathy, axon loss, field loss, blindness","Raised pressure detaches the retina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The irreversibility is the point — the axons do not come back, which is why the whole of therapy is aimed at preventing progression rather than recovering vision. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Apraclonidine is highly ionised at physiological pH while brimonidine is more lipophilic. What is the practical consequence?","choices":["Ionisation increases corneal penetration and local potency","Lipophilicity prevents systemic absorption","The difference is purely one of shelf stability","Ionisation limits blood-brain barrier penetration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A charged molecule crosses membranes poorly, so it stays where it is put. Brimonidine, being more lipophilic, is also the one with less allergic conjunctivitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A patient with asthma needs a topical beta blocker for glaucoma. Which agent is safer, and why?","choices":["Timolol, because it is beta-1 selective","Levobunolol, because it is more efficacious","Carteolol, because it has no systemic absorption","Betaxolol, because it is beta-1 selective"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 receptors mediate bronchodilation, so blocking them raises airway resistance. Betaxolol is the beta-1 selective option on this list; carteolol, timolol and levobunolol are non-selective. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 67-68"},{"q":"Glaucoma drugs work by one of two strategies. What are they?","choices":["Decreasing aqueous production or reducing episcleral venous pressure","Increasing outflow or reducing optic nerve metabolic demand","Reducing production or increasing outflow","Dilating the pupil or constricting the ciliary muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every agent in this section sorts into one or the other, and some do both. Sorting a drug by which side it is on is the fastest way to remember the whole class list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How do ophthalmic beta blockers lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["They increase outflow through the trabecular meshwork","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary epithelium","They contract the ciliary muscle","They block beta receptors in the ciliary epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Less catecholamine activation means less cyclic AMP, and less aqueous is made. They act on the production side, not on drainage. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Why is more than one agent often needed to control intraocular pressure?","choices":["Targeting production and outflow together","Tolerance develops to every single agent within weeks","Each agent can only be used at half dose","Single agents are contraindicated in open angle disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Combinations are described as synergistic for exactly that reason, and they also cut the number of drops a patient has to manage, which helps compliance. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 64 and 73"},{"q":"In which patients are ophthalmic alpha-2 agonists contraindicated, and why?","choices":["Patients with asthma, because of bronchoconstriction","Patients with sulfonamide allergy","Children under two, from apnoea risk","Patients over 65, because of falls"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A hard age contraindication, and the mechanism is the same central alpha-2 effect that makes imidazoline ingestion dangerous in toddlers. Worth holding alongside that question. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which cosmetic adverse effects should a patient starting a prostaglandin analogue be warned about?","choices":["Permanent pupillary constriction and myopia","Eyelash and iris colour change, with hyperaemia","Bitter taste and stinging on instillation","Bradycardia and wheeze"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The eyelash effect is marketed as a product in its own right, and iris darkening can be permanent — which is why it is a consent conversation rather than a footnote, particularly when only one eye is treated. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How do ophthalmic alpha-2 agonists lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["They reduce production and increase outflow","They block beta receptors in the ciliary epithelium","They contract the ciliary muscle to open the trabecular meshwork","They analogue prostaglandin F2-alpha at prostaglandin receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They work on both sides of the equation, which is why they appear in both lists — decreased production and increased outflow. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How does pilocarpine lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["It blocks muscarinic receptors, dilating the pupil","It activates muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle","It reduces aqueous production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase","It analogues prostaglandin F2-alpha"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contracting the ciliary muscle pulls on the trabecular meshwork and opens the drainage route — an outflow drug achieved by a muscle action. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A patient is already on timolol and needs a second agent. Which combination product makes sense, and what is the general advantage of combinations?","choices":["Brimonidine with timolol, targeting both mechanisms","A second beta blocker, because the effect is additive","Dorzolamide with timolol, because both increase outflow","Pilocarpine alone, because miotics are best tolerated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Given the drug someone is already on, the question is what second-line agent helps most. Adding an alpha agonist to a beta blocker hits both production and outflow, and fewer drops improves compliance. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A patient has an intraocular pressure of 26 mmHg with a normal optic nerve and no visual field loss. What is this called?","choices":["Open angle glaucoma","Angle closure glaucoma","Normal tension glaucoma","Ocular hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pressure above the normal 10 to 21 mmHg but with no optic nerve damage and no field loss. It is a risk factor rather than a disease, and whether to treat depends on the other risk factors. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which class is first line for open angle glaucoma, and how often is it given?","choices":["Beta blockers, twice daily","Prostaglandin analogues, once daily","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, three times daily","Cholinergic agonists, four times daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are the most commonly used glaucoma medications and the first line agents. Once daily is the whole dose — and more often is actively worse, not merely unnecessary. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 64 and 66"}]};
